\chapter{Repository layout and reproducible publishing}
\begin{description}
\item[\api{mull/mullier}] C++ and D passes, public entry points, embedded rules.
\item[\api{mull/rewrite}] Authored analysis/transformation S-expressions and their embedder.
\item[\api{mull/stdlib}] Discoverable MuLL packages and aggregate installer facades.
\item[\api{mull/native}] C++ and D implementations of the five native base packages.
\item[\api{mull/bridge}] MuLIB's generated inventory, typed adapters, and shared C ABI.
\end{description}
This layout separates compiler inputs, language packages, and compiled host code.
An installed application supplies its package root explicitly, commonly
\api{share/mull/stdlib}. Default embedded rewrite rules need no filesystem access.
Host-selected rules can instead be loaded from \api{share/mull/rewrite}.

\section{The generated reference parts}
The repository-root \api{manual/} describes MuLIB. The toolkit's separate
\api{mudsl/manual/} supplies the first 37 chapters. Both conversions use the
provided \api{md2latex.py} parser through a book-specific renderer that selects
minted languages and promotes headings. \api{typeset-manuals.py} renders all
root Markdown manuals with their document titles, source provenance, and linked
sections. Reading guides precede the alphabetic module reference. HTML disclosure
sections are expanded for print, and multipage tables repeat their headers.
Generated fragments live below \api{book/build/generated}.

\section{Typography as part of the build}
MuLL has its own Pygments lexer: declaration/control keywords, short-circuit
operators, byte-string escapes, error interpolation, and both comment forms.
The build stages the plugin under \api{build/python}; it does not modify the
Python installation. LuaLaTeX uses minted, microtypography, shaded breakable code,
running headings, and linked contents. Source appendices are read directly with
\api{inputminted}, so they cannot drift from the repository sources.

\begin{code}
cmake -S mudsl -B build/mudsl -DMULL_ENABLE_MULIB=OFF
cmake --build build/mudsl -j4
ctest --test-dir build/mudsl --output-on-failure
make -C mudsl/book export
make -C mudsl/book check
make -C mudsl/book
\end{code}
The book requires Python with Pygments, LuaLaTeX, latexmk, minted's
Python executable, and the TeX Gyre/DejaVu fonts. The Makefile supplies shell
escape for highlighting and a local plugin path. Edit authored Markdown or
LaTeX sources, regenerate, and verify the result; generated files are not the
place to repair a source-level mistake.
